course: dictionary
lesson: 2213
title: Couple: two, pairs and joining
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A couple is two people or two things that are together. In the story, «ten royal children ornamented with hearts, jumping merrily along hand in hand, in couples» means they went two by two, in pairs. The clue is the word «hand in hand», because you hold hands with one partner.
explain: Look at the words around a word that has more than one meaning. «Fish should get only as much food as they eat in a couple of minutes.» Here «a couple of» means about two, or just a few. Try the meaning in the sentence: «in about two minutes» still makes sense, so the meaning fits.
explain: Couple can also be a verb, a doing word. To couple means to join two things together, like train cars. Ask yourself: is it one pair of things, or is someone joining things? The sentence tells you which meaning to use.
fact: A couple means two people or two things that are together.
fact: A couple of things means two of them, and in everyday talk it can mean a few.
fact: To couple means to join two things together, like train cars.
fact: «In couples» means two by two, in pairs.
fact: Maths tip: if there are 5 couples, there are 5 × 2 = 10 people.
exercise: «They walked in couples, hand in hand, across the bridge.» What does in couples mean? | Without any shoes | *In pairs, two by two | In a long line, one by one | Very fast
exercise: Maya says «a couple of minutes» means exactly 200 seconds. What is the best answer? | *It usually means about two minutes, or a few minutes | It means a very long time, like an hour | It means 20 minutes | It means exactly 200 seconds
exercise: «The driver coupled the carriages together.» What does coupled mean here? | Painted them | *Joined them together | Counted them to two | Split them apart
test: If there are 4 couples dancing, how many people are dancing? | 6 | *8 | 4 | 2
test: «A couple of cups of milk» means about how many cups? | One hundred | *Two | Zero | Ten
exam: Which word means to join two things together? | Divide | Scatter | *Couple | Count
